# Colour, images, and icons

Read [`DESIGN.md`](../../DESIGN.md) for theme tokens and brand pops.

## Principle

Every colour, image, and icon has a **job**. If you cannot name the job in one phrase, remove it.

| Job | Examples in this cabinet |
|---|---|
| **Distinction** | Player vs opponent, suit colour, legal vs illegal |
| **Hierarchy** | Muted group labels, ink for primary actions |
| **State** | `--you`, `--danger`, `--wait`, selected seat |
| **Evidence** | Score, room code, whose turn — must stay legible without colour alone |
| **Recognition** | Brand mark, Kenney art, game-specific board identity |

Colour is **redundant**, not the sole channel. Pair tint with weight, border, position, or copy.

## Restraint

- Theme neutrals carry the shell; brand pops mark state and rare emphasis.
- Boards keep saturated game colour; chrome stays quiet.
- No indigo-violet defaults, purple-blue gradients, or vibecode accent stacks (see `anti-slop.mdc`).
- Yellow (`--brand-yellow`) is fill/mark, not body text on light surfaces.

## Icons

Icons share one language: simple, readable at tap size, same stroke weight family.

- Settings, theme cycle, motion cycle: functional, not decorative.
- **Sometimes no icon** — a word or the board itself is enough.
- Do not sprinkle icons to fake structure (feature-card grids, eyebrow pills).

## Images

- Default art: Kenney CC0 via shared tabletop kit.
- Brand mark on the wordmark only; games supply their own board personality.
- No stock illustration heroes, orbs, blobs, or page gradients as chrome filler.

## Visual abundance

More boxes, badges, and ribbons ≠ clearer UI. Prefer one honest surface over layered faux depth.

Conflicts: favour **intentionality** and **GamesCabinet undertone** (handmade cabinet, not SaaS landing) over ornamental chrome.
